<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <a class="tag" href="/guides/">Guides</a>
      <h1>How to Verify a Download Before You Install It: Checksums and Signatures</h1>
      <p class="meta">Published October 19, 2026 &middot; ToolStep Editorial Team</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      A large installer, a firmware image, a set of model weights, an archive pulled from a mirror at 2am. You click, wait, and open it — and you have no way of knowing whether the bytes that landed on disk are the bytes the project produced. Verification closes that gap in under a minute. Here is how to do it on Windows, macOS, and Linux, what a signature adds over a hash, and — importantly — what neither one proves.
    </p>

    <div class="answer-box">
      <h2>The two checks people mix up</h2>
      <p><strong>Integrity:</strong> the file was not truncated or corrupted in transit. A checksum comparison answers this, and it catches the most common real-world failure, which is simply a bad download.</p>
      <p><strong>Origin:</strong> the file really came from the publisher rather than from someone who substituted it. A checksum published on the same page as the download only partly answers this. A cryptographic signature checked against a public key you established by another route answers it properly.</p>
      <p>Neither check tells you the software is free of malicious code or bugs. It tells you that you have exactly the file the publisher shipped, and — with signatures — reasonable confidence about who that was.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Before you start: get the expected value from the right place</h2>
    <p>Look for a file named something like <code>SHA256SUMS</code>, a "verified hashes" table on the release page, or a checksum listed next to each download link. Two habits matter more than the tool you use:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Compare the same algorithm.</strong> If the publisher posts SHA-256, compute SHA-256. Older projects sometimes publish only MD5 or SHA-1, which still detect corruption but are no longer good enough where deliberate substitution is the concern.</li>
      <li><strong>Get the expected value over a channel you did not get the file from</strong> when one exists — the project's main site rather than a random mirror, or a signed announcement rather than the download page itself. This is the step that upgrades integrity checking into origin checking.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Windows</h2>
    <p>Two options ship with the system. PowerShell's <code>Get-FileHash</code> is the one to prefer; <code>certutil</code> works from a plain command prompt.</p>
    <pre><code>Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 -Path C:\Users\you\Downloads\setup.exe</code></pre>
    <pre><code>certutil -hashfile C:\Users\you\Downloads\setup.exe SHA256</code></pre>
    <p><code>Get-FileHash</code> defaults to SHA256, prints uppercase hex, and is documented in the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash">Microsoft PowerShell reference</a>. Its output has spaces removed in the hash itself, while <code>certutil</code> wraps the value across lines — if a comparison fails, check you are not just looking at formatting.</p>

    <h3>Verify a detached signature on Windows</h3>
    <p>Windows has no built-in GPG. If a project publishes a <code>.sig</code> or <code>.asc</code> file, install GnuPG (or the GPG4Win suite) and use the same commands as the Linux section below. A signature is only as good as the public key you check it with, so import keys from the publisher's stated fingerprint rather than from a keyserver alone.</p>

    <h2>macOS</h2>
    <p>macOS ships <code>shasum</code>, which takes the digest algorithm as an option:</p>
    <pre><code>shasum -a 256 ~/Downloads/tool.tar.gz</code></pre>
    <p>The <code>openssl</code> binary is also present on recent macOS releases and can produce the same digest:</p>
    <pre><code>openssl dgst -sha256 ~/Downloads/tool.tar.gz</code></pre>
    <p>Note that macOS does not include GNU coreutils' <code>sha256sum</code> under that name by default. If you copy commands from Linux documentation and the command is not found, use <code>shasum -a 256</code> instead of installing anything.</p>

    <h2>Linux</h2>
    <p>Coreutils provides <code>sha256sum</code> — see the <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sha256sum.1.html">sha256sum manual page</a> and the wider <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/index.html">GNU Coreutils manual</a>:</p>
    <pre><code>sha256sum tool.tar.gz</code></pre>
    <p>The real value on Linux is batch verification. When a project ships a checksums file alongside several artefacts, run it from the download directory:</p>
    <pre><code>sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS</code></pre>
    <p>Each line is reported as <code>OK</code> or <code>FAILED</code>. Distributions publish hashes the same way: <a href="https://help.ubuntu.com/community/UbuntuHashes">Ubuntu's official hash listings</a> and the <a href="https://www.debian.org/security/">Debian security pages</a> both show how publishers present digests for images and updates.</p>

    <AdsterraBanner pageType="guides" />

    <h2>Signatures: the part that establishes who published it</h2>
    <p>A detached signature is a file created by signing the digest with the publisher's private key. You verify it with their public key:</p>
    <pre><code>gpg --verify tool.tar.gz.asc tool.tar.gz
gpg --list-keys --fingerprint PUBLISHER_KEY_ID</code></pre>
    <p>A good verification line reports a valid signature and a fingerprint. Compare that fingerprint to the one the project publishes in a location that would be harder to swap — its main site, its documentation, or a previous copy you kept. See the <a href="https://www.gnupg.org/documentation/">GnuPG documentation</a> for option details, since flag behaviour differs between versions.</p>
    <p>The hierarchy of trust, weakest to strongest, looks like this: no check at all, then a hash from the same page as the file, then a hash from a channel you separated from the download, then a signature from a key whose fingerprint you confirmed independently. Each step costs a little time and rules out a different class of failure.</p>

    <h2>When the check fails: what to do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Re-download before concluding anything.</strong> A mismatch most often means an interrupted transfer, a browser that saved a partially written file, or an archive that was re-extracted over itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Check you hashed the right file.</strong> Timestamps and duplicate names in Downloads are a frequent cause of false alarms.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the algorithm and encoding.</strong> MD5 against SHA-256, uppercase against lowercase, and a checksum file with the wrong filename all produce mismatches that look like attacks.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm the expected value is current.</strong> Projects rebuild releases; a hash bookmarked last month may not match today's artefact.</li>
      <li><strong>If a re-download still mismatches, stop.</strong> Do not "just try installing it". Fetch from the project's primary site, and if it still differs, treat the copy you have as untrusted and report it to the project.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warn-box">
      <h2>Limits worth stating plainly</h2>
      <p>A verification pass is not a safety verdict. Malicious or simply buggy code signed by the real publisher still passes every check in this guide. Antivirus and reputation tools cover a different risk, and neither replaces the other — our look at <a href="/best/best-antivirus-software/">the best antivirus software</a> explains what those products actually catch.</p>
      <p>Also: verification proves what a file <em>is</em>, not what it will do. An installer can be genuine and still request elevated privileges, phone home, or bundle components you did not choose. Read the permission prompts. A checksum cannot protect you from consent you gave willingly.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>A practical minimum, if you do nothing else</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Download from the project's own domain, over the site's HTTPS link — not from a search-ad result or a "download portal".</li>
      <li>Compute SHA-256 on the file and compare it character-for-character with the value on that same site.</li>
      <li>Where the project publishes a signature, verify it against a key whose fingerprint you saw somewhere else.</li>
      <li>Keep the checksum file next to the archive if you store it long term — it is also how you detect bit rot later, alongside the health checks in <a href="/guides/how-to-check-drive-health/">our drive-health guide</a>.</li>
    </ol>

